-- store_projection_toy.{mm0,mm1} — a *proved* toy model of the memory
-- projection stated in `mm0/store_projection.mm0`.
--
-- WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. `store_projection.mm0` states two claims (load and
-- store project onto the ISA model at width 8) and proves neither, because the
-- real proof needs a semantics for `jit_store_i64`/`jit_load_i64` and a
-- simulation between two state types. Those are not in this repo. What *can* be
-- settled today is the shape: pick concrete operations for the two sides and
-- check that the projection then goes through, from named and cited memory
-- axioms, with nothing assumed about the compiler.
--
-- WHAT IS PROVED HERE (9 theorems, 0 sorry):
--
--   toy_init_unset            a fresh memory reads `unset` at every address
--   toy_jit_store_visible     a store through the JIT's definition makes the
--                             value visible at the written address
--   toy_isa_store_visible     the same for the model's definition at width 8
--   toy_store_projection      the two definitions agree at the written address
--   toy_load_projection       the two load definitions return the same value
--   toy_jit_store_load_other  a load from a *different* address after a store
--                             returns what was there before the store
--   toy_isa_store_load_other  the same for the model's definition at width 8
--   toy_jit_two_cells_preserved   two stores at *distinct* addresses both stay
--                             readable: the second does not clobber the first
--   toy_isa_two_cells_preserved   the same for the model's definition
--
-- The memory is **two cells**: `put` writes the free cell, evicting the older
-- one, so a second store at a different address leaves the first address's
-- content intact. That is why the store-then-load-elsewhere theorems say "the
-- value before the store" rather than "unset" — with a single cell the earlier
-- write would be gone, and with two it survives. What the toy is deliberately
-- *silent* about is eviction: after three distinct stores the oldest address
-- may have been dropped, and no axiom here says which. That is a real gap in
-- the model, not an oversight in the proof, and it is the same gap the real
-- obligation has in a different form (nobody has proved how many outstanding
-- cells the lowering keeps).
--
-- WHAT IS *NOT* PROVED, and it is the whole distance to W9.3: that the emitted
-- Cranelift instruction computes `jit_store_i64`, that Sail's `vmem_write` at
-- width 8 computes `isa_store_w8`, that an aligned address stays in the linear
-- space, or that the fault branch is never taken there. Here `put`, `get` and
-- `veq` are uninterpreted and the two sides are *defined to be the same term*,
-- so the theorems say "if both sides are this operation, they agree" — which is
-- true, cheap, and exactly as strong as it looks. The real content sits in the
-- axioms below and those are cited assumptions, not theorems.
--
-- Keeping this file separate from `store_projection.mm0` is deliberate: that
-- artifact must contain no `axiom` and no `theorem` so it cannot quietly become
-- a proof of something it did not prove, and `scripts/verify-invariants`
-- enforces exactly that on it. Proofs live here, claims live there, and this
-- file cites the claim it models by digest.
--
-- theory coverage: used=put,get,init,unset,veq,vne unused=none
-- cites: mm0/store_projection.mm0 sha256=2b94039df2faccf5d4be102dfec17e2e72260271b64e6495c15249465ef86df7

provable sort Prop;
delimiter $ ( ) $;

-- ── The toy memory: two cells ────────────────────────────────────────────
-- `Mem` is a two-cell memory, `Val` is both address and value; both are
-- uninterpreted, so nothing here is 64-bit, aligned or linear. The two cells
-- are not exposed as a constructor: `put` and `get` are the interface, and what
-- the two-cell capacity *means* is stated axiom by axiom below rather than
-- derived from a data layout.
sort Val;
sort Mem;
-- The memory handed to the program: both cells empty.
term init: Mem;
-- The content of an empty cell.
term unset: Val;
term put (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem;
term get (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val;
-- Address inequality, uninterpreted like `veq`; the axioms below take it as a
-- hypothesis rather than deciding it.
term vne (x y: Val): Prop;

-- Value equality, as an uninterpreted predicate with the three facts the
-- proofs chain through. Stating these instead of importing an equality theory
-- is what keeps this file's coverage line honest: `veq` is the only equality
-- used, and it is assumed, not derived. `veq_trans` is what lets "read
-- unchanged" and "read-after-write" compose into "the earlier store survived".
term veq (x y: Val): Prop;
axiom veq_refl (x: Val): $ veq x x $;
axiom veq_trans (x y z: Val) (h1: $ veq x y $) (h2: $ veq y z $): $ veq x z $;
axiom vne_sym (x y: Val) (h: $ vne x y $): $ vne y x $;

-- The toy's memory semantics: a fresh memory is empty; a write is visible at
-- the address written; and a write at one address leaves every *other*
-- address's content as it was — the two-cell capacity, stated where it is
-- used. All four are cited assumptions.
axiom get_init (a: Val): $ veq (get init a) unset $;
axiom at_put (m: Mem) (a v: Val): $ veq (get (put m a v) a) v $;
axiom at_put_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (put m a v) b) (get m b) $;

-- ── The two sides of the projection, concretely ──────────────────────────
-- The emitted instruction's effect and the model's effect at width 8, both
-- defined as the same write. In the real projection these are *different*
-- definitions (Cranelift lowering vs. Sail `vmem_write`), and proving them equal
-- is the work this toy skips by construction.
def jit_store_i64 (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem = $ put m a v $;
def isa_store_w8 (m: Mem) (a v: Val): Mem = $ put m a v $;
def jit_load_i64 (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val = $ get m a $;
def isa_load_w8 (m: Mem) (a: Val): Val = $ get m a $;

-- ── What the model proves ────────────────────────────────────────────────
-- Note that these are theorems about the *definitions*, not restatements of
-- the axioms: the goals are written in terms of `jit_store_i64` /
-- `isa_store_w8` and the proofs go through only because the definitions unfold.
theorem toy_init_unset (a: Val):
  $ veq (get init a) unset $;

theorem toy_jit_store_visible (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) a) v $;

theorem toy_isa_store_visible (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) a) v $;

theorem toy_store_projection (m: Mem) (a v: Val):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) a) (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) a) $;

theorem toy_load_projection (m: Mem) (a: Val):
  $ veq (jit_load_i64 m a) (isa_load_w8 m a) $;

-- Store, then load from an address other than the one written: the second cell
-- holds the value the store did not touch, so the read is unchanged. The
-- hypothesis `vne a b` is the toy's stand-in for the real claim's
-- `aligned8`/linearity side conditions: something about the addresses must be
-- known, and here it is the weakest thing that makes the answer determinate.
theorem toy_jit_store_load_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 m a v) b) (get m b) $;

theorem toy_isa_store_load_other (m: Mem) (a b v: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 m a v) b) (get m b) $;

-- And the case the second cell exists for: store at `a`, then store at a
-- *different* address `b`, then read `a` — the earlier store is still there.
-- This is where the axioms have to compose: "read unchanged" turns the outer
-- read into the inner one, and read-after-write supplies the value.
theorem toy_jit_two_cells_preserved
  (m: Mem) (a b va vb: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (jit_store_i64 (jit_store_i64 m a va) b vb) a) va $;

theorem toy_isa_two_cells_preserved
  (m: Mem) (a b va vb: Val) (h: $ vne a b $):
  $ veq (get (isa_store_w8 (isa_store_w8 m a va) b vb) a) va $;
